When converting a string to a double using `std::stod`, if the value in the string is outside the range that can be represented by a double, `std::stod` will throw a `std::out_of_range` exception. If the string does not represent a valid number, it will throw a `std::invalid_argument` exception.

Here is how you can check for out-of-bounds values:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <stdexcept>

int main() {
    std::string str = "1e400"; // Example: too large for double

    try {
        double value = std::stod(str);
        std::cout << "Converted value: " << value << std::endl;
    } catch (const std::out_of_range& e) {
        std::cerr << "Error: Value is out of range for double." << std::endl;
    } catch (const std::invalid_argument& e) {
        std::cerr << "Error: Invalid input string." << std::endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

