Your current Trie implementation uses a `vector<Node*>` of size 26 for each node, which is not memory efficient because each node allocates a vector (with its own heap allocation and bookkeeping) even if most child pointers are unused. Here are some ways to make your code more memory efficient:

1. **Use a fixed-size array instead of vector:**  
   Since the number of possible children is always 26 (for lowercase English letters), you can use a plain C-style array or `std::array<Node*, 26>`. This avoids the overhead of `vector` (size, capacity, allocator, etc.) and stores the pointers inline.

   ```cpp
   struct Node {
