occurs because in your code, you are attempting to use `IList` as if it were a non-generic interface, but the compiler is interpreting it as the generic `IList<T>`, which requires a type argument (e.g., `IList<int>`). This typically happens if you have a `using System.Collections.Generic;` directive, which brings the generic `IList<T>` into scope, and you write `IList` without specifying which one you mean.

**2. Does it make sense that it is faulty? What is the logic behind the issue Unity is declaring?**

Yes, it makes sense. In C#, `IList` exists in two forms:
- `System.Collections.IList` (non-generic, no type parameter)
- `System.Collections.Generic.IList<T>` (generic, requires a type parameter)

If you write `IList` without a namespace, and you have both `using System.Collections;` and `using System.Collections.Generic;`, the compiler may not know which one you mean, or may default to the generic one, which requires a type argument. Attempting to use `IList` without a type argument when the generic version is in scope causes the error.

**3. How to fix it, and why will it work?**

You want to handle any kind of list dynamically, so you should use the non-generic `System.Collections.IList` interface, which all `List<T>` types implement. By explicitly casting to `System.Collections.IList`, you avoid the ambiguity and do not need to specify a type parameter.

**Corrected code:**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections;
using System.Collections.Generic;

private static Dictionary<string, object> SaveCollection(object obj)
{
    var collectionValues = new Dictionary<string, object>();
    var type = obj.GetType();

    if (type.IsArray)
    {
        var array = (Array)obj;
        for (int i = 0; i < array.Length; i++)
        {
            collectionValues[i.ToString()] = array.GetValue(i);
        }
    }
    else if (typeof(IList).IsAssignableFrom(type))
    {
        var list = (IList)obj; // Explicitly non-generic IList
        for (int i = 0; i < list.Count; i++)
        {
            collectionValues[i.ToString()] = list[i];
        }
    }

    return collectionValues;
}
```

